{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Living Estuary",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Marguerite Aldous",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Where the River Gives Up",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "ch:tides",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "An estuary is a place of argument. Twice a day the sea pushes inland, and twice a day the river pushes back; everything that lives between the two has to cope with water that changes its mind. Walk the mud of the Wenlow Estuary at low tide and you stand on the floor of a sea that left an hour ago and will return before supper.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This book follows one such estuary through a single year. It is not a field manual, although it borrows from several; it is closer to a long walk with a good companion who stops often to point at things. The first chapter explains the physical machine, the tide and the salt gradient it drives. The second looks at the animals that have made a living inside that machine.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The tidal machine",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Wenlow rises on peat moorland and runs forty kilometres to the sea, but its last twelve kilometres belong to the tide. At spring tides the water at the mouth rises and falls by 5.8 metres; at the head of the estuary, where the channel narrows and the bed shoals, the range is larger still, an effect known as funnelling",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Narrowing channels squeeze the tidal wave upward. In the most extreme cases a steep front, a tidal bore, runs upriver ahead of the flood.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "Footnote"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Hydrographers estimate the volume of water that enters and leaves on each tide with the tidal prism,",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "P = A_{\\mathrm{s}}\\, R,",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:prism",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "where ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "A_{\\mathrm{s}}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the surface area of the estuary at mid-tide and ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "R",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the tidal range. For the Wenlow, ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "A_{\\mathrm{s}} = 14.2",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " km",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "^2",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " and ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "R = 4.1",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " m on an average tide, so Equation ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:prism",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " gives a prism of roughly ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "5.8\\times10^{7}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " cubic metres. That is about two hundred times the volume the river alone delivers over the same six hours, which is why the estuary belongs more to the sea than to the river that named it.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Salt and mud",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Salinity falls steadily from the sea to the head of the estuary, but the transition is never tidy. On a falling tide, fresher water rides over saltier water like oil on vinegar; on a spring flood the two mix violently. Biologists therefore divide an estuary into zones by the salinity an animal can expect to meet, not by distance from the sea. Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:zones",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " gives the zones used throughout this book.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Zone",
          "Salinity (PSU)",
          "Distance from mouth (km)"
        ],
        [
          "Marine",
          "30 to 35",
          "0 to 3"
        ],
        [
          "Polyhaline",
          "18 to 30",
          "3 to 6"
        ],
        [
          "Mesohaline",
          "5 to 18",
          "6 to 9"
        ],
        [
          "Oligohaline",
          "0.5 to 5",
          "9 to 12"
        ],
        [
          "Tidal fresh",
          "below 0.5",
          "12 to 14"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Salinity zones of the Wenlow Estuary at mean river flow.",
      "label": "tab:zones",
      "alignment": "lcc",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "merges": [],
      "header": true,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The mud itself is a product of the same mixing. Fine clay particles carried by the river clump together when they meet salt water, sink, and settle in the quiet water behind the sandbanks. Over centuries this has built the broad flats that make the estuary so rich (",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "harrow1998"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": "). A single square metre of healthy mud can hold ten thousand small worms and snails, and these in turn feed most of the estuary's birds.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Those Who Live in the Mud",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "ch:animals",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Life in the estuary is built on a simple bargain: tolerate the changes, and the food is enormous. Few species accept the terms. Those that do tend to be present in huge numbers, and the Wenlow is no exception.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Burrowers and grazers",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Three animals do most of the work on the mudflats.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The ragworm, which burrows in the mesohaline mud and surfaces to feed at high tide;",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "the mud snail, which grazes the film of algae on the surface and leaves tracks visible from the sea wall;",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "the baltic tellin, a thin pink bivalve that vacuums the mud with a long siphon.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        }
      ],
      "type": "List"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Their numbers are not constant. Surveys by the Wenlow Estuary Trust show a marked winter fall, followed by a spring recovery that tracks the return of the algae (Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:density",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ").",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Species",
          "February",
          "May",
          "August"
        ],
        [
          "Ragworm",
          "2100",
          "3400",
          "5200"
        ],
        [
          "Mud snail",
          "800",
          "2900",
          "6100"
        ],
        [
          "Baltic tellin",
          "1500",
          "1900",
          "2300"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Mean density (animals per square metre) on the central flats, 2022 survey.",
      "label": "tab:density",
      "alignment": "lrrr",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "merges": [],
      "header": true,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The birds that follow the tide",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Every one of those animals is somebody's lunch. By October the flats fill with waders: knot, dunlin, redshank and curlew, each with a bill shaped for a different depth of mud. A curlew probes ten centimetres down for ragworm; a dunlin, with a short straight bill, picks tellins from the top two. They share the flats without much competition, a division of labour that ecologists call niche partitioning (",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "harrow1998",
            "vance2009"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ").",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The birds keep to the tide's timetable and not the clock's. Each feeds as the flats are uncovered, roosts on the saltmarsh at high water, and returns when the mud reappears. A winter day on the estuary therefore has two long meals and two long rests, offset by about fifty minutes from the day before.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "That daily rhythm is fragile. A walker with a dog on the flats at the wrong moment can send three hundred birds into the air, and each unplanned flight burns energy that a wader in January can ill afford",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Vance estimated that a single disturbance costs a wintering dunlin about four per cent of its daily energy intake.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "Footnote"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The simplest conservation measure in the estuary turns out to be an honest tide table pinned to the car park noticeboard.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "text": "\\begin{thebibliography}{99}\n\\bibitem{harrow1998} T. Harrow, \\emph{Mud, Salt and Sediment}. Fenmouth University Press, 1998.\n\\bibitem{vance2009} R. Vance and L. Okoro, Disturbance and energy budgets of wintering waders. \\emph{Journal of Estuarine Birds} \\textbf{14} (2009), 211--226.\n\\end{thebibliography}",
      "type": "RawLatex"
    }
